    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2 Homotopy of Cp(G)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 656.511, 491.488, 682.878 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this section, we review some of the standard facts of order complexes of posets. See the ﬁrst two parts of Smith’s book[9] for more details."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 555.339, 491.401, 654.078 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Let (P, ≤) be a poset (short for partially ordered set). The order complex ∆P of P consists of all ﬁnite chains in P, including the empty set. The chain of length i in P consisting of i + 1 elements is called an i-simplex in ∆P. In particular, the empty set ∅ is the (−1)-simplex. It is well-known that such (abstract) simplicial complex ∆P can be embedded into a Euclidean topological space as a subspace denoted by |∆P|, so-called the geometric realization of ∆P. We abbreviate |∆P| as |P| here."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 468.711, 491.399, 552.919 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "A poset map f : (P, ≤P) → (Q, ≤Q) of posets P, Q, is a set-theoretical map such that x ≤P y implies f(x) ≤Q f(y). It is routine to check that a poset map f : P → Q naturally induces a simplicial map ∆f between order complexes ∆P and ∆Q, and f also induces a continuous map |f| : |P| → |Q|. The following lemma gives a suﬃcient condition for the homotopy between two continuous maps induced by poset maps."
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 8,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 220.827, 491.569, 457.278 ],
    "numbering style" : "arabic numbers",
    "number of list items" : 2,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 416.379, 491.569, 457.278 ],
      "font" : "SFBX1200",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Lemma 2. [9, Lemma 3.1.7] Suppose that f, g : P → Q satisfy f, g are poset maps with f ≤ g, that is, f(x) ≤ g(x) for each x ∈ P. Then f and g are homotopic, that is, |f|, |g| : |P| → |Q| are homotopic.",
      "kids" : [ {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 1,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 102.84, 335.152, 491.435, 404.958 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1200",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Recall that topological spaces X and Y are homotopy equivalent if there are continuous maps f : X → Y and g : Y → X whose compositions satisfy the homotopies g ◦ f ≃ IdX and f ◦ g ≃ IdY . For posets P, Q, P and Q are said to be homotopy-equivalent if |P| and |Q| are homotopy-equivalent, simply denoted by P ≃ Q."
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 2,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 118.439, 293.668, 468.805, 332.719 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1200",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "For a prime p and a group G, we denote Ip(G) = P1 ∩ P2 · · · ∩ Ps | Pi ∈ Sylp(G) for all 1 ≤ i ≤ s, and s ≥ 1"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 3,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 270.231, 422.051, 282.198 ],
        "font" : "SFRM1200",
        "font size" : 11.955,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "by the set of all intersections of some Sylow p-subgroups of G."
      } ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 220.827, 395.879, 258.798 ],
      "font" : "SFBX1200",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "Lemma 3. Let G be a group and p be a prime. Write C (Ip(G)) = {Hx | H ∈ Ip(G), x ∈ G}.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 102.839, 172.108, 491.342, 208.278 ],
    "font" : "SFTI1200",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Then Cp(G) is homotopy-equivalent to C(Ip(G)). Proof. For each Hx ∈ Cp(G), we deﬁne a map f from Cp(G) to C(Ip(G)) by"
